  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What does assets under management mean?",
      "answer": "Assets under management (AUM) is the total dollar amount of client assets an investment manager oversees. SEC Form ADV Part 2A requires investment advisers to disclose this figure in their narrative brochure to describe the scale of their advisory business. Investor.gov notes that ongoing advisory fees are typically based on the total value of all assets held in your account."
    },
    {
      "question": "What are assets under management fees?",
      "answer": "Assets under management fees are advisory charges calculated as a percentage of the total value of assets in your managed account. The SEC Form ADV Part 2A requires firms to describe their fee schedules, explain whether fees are negotiable, and state how payments are deducted. Investor.gov notes that asset-based fees may cost more than transactional commissions but support continuous portfolio monitoring."
    },
    {
      "question": "How does assets under management work?",
      "answer": "Assets under management functions as both a measure of portfolio size and a basis for ongoing advisory billing. An investment adviser monitors your investments over time and assesses a fee tied to the total account balance. Under Form ADV Part 2A guidelines, the adviser must specify its methods of analysis, investment strategies, and how other costs like custody and fund fees apply."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is investment management the same as wealth management?",
      "answer": "Investment management focuses specifically on buying, selling, monitoring, and adjusting securities within a portfolio. Our comparison of a [financial advisor vs wealth manager](/compare/financial-advisor-vs-wealth-manager/) explains how industry titles and broader service bundles differ in scope. This guide addresses investment management and the choice between managing securities yourself or hiring an investment adviser."
    }
  ],
